#set document(
  title: "Math via Helvetica",
  author: "Mosaic contributors",
  language: "en",
)

#set page(
  paper: "A4",
  margin: 24mm,
  numbering: "bottom-center",
)

#set text(
  font: "Helvetica",
  size: 11pt,
  leading: 1.35,
)

= Math via the Base14 path

`#set text(font: "Helvetica")` routes through Mosaic's Base14
encoder, which layers a per-document `/Differences` array on top
of `WinAnsiEncoding` to reach the AFM glyphs beyond WinAnsi. The
ten math symbols Adobe ships in every Core 14 Latin AFM are
available without embedding any font — the outlines come from
the reader's built-in Helvetica.

Inequalities: a ≤ b, x ≥ y, p ≠ q.

Calculus: ∂f/∂x = 0, ∑ a = 1, √n ≥ 0.

Geometry: ∆ABC, optional steps marked ◊.

The minus operator (a − b) uses U+2212, distinct from the ASCII
hyphen-minus (a - b). The fraction slash (1 ⁄ 2) uses U+2044.

== Coverage

This path covers exactly ten math glyphs — `lessequal`,
`greaterequal`, `notequal`, `partialdiff`, `summation`,
`radical`, `minus`, `Delta`, `lozenge`, `fraction` — the AGL-
named glyphs every Core 14 Latin AFM ships. Symbols outside that
list (infinity, forall, exists, element-of, nabla, product,
oplus, otimes, sub/superscripts, calligraphic and blackletter
alphabets) require font embedding and are unreachable through
the `/Differences` path. The eventual fix is bundling Noto Sans
Math with per-glyph font fallback so math codepoints route
through it while body text stays in the primary family —
tracked as a follow-up to issue #9.

== Caveat

Glyph coverage past WinAnsi depends on each PDF reader's chosen
Helvetica substitute, not just on Mosaic emitting a correct
`/Differences` declaration. Even within the poppler family,
different installs (GNOME Papers vs Evince vs zathura) can pick
different substitute fonts via fontconfig and end up with
different glyph coverage — `lozenge` (◊) in particular is
prone to being absent. Adobe Reader has separately been observed
to mis-render `/Differences`-mapped glyphs by splicing in
spacing-substitution composition (Romanian comma-below in
particular). If you target a specific reader and rely on any
specific glyph from the AGL math subset, verify the rendering on
that reader before shipping.
